# Credentials and identity values for the launchd services.
#
# Copy this file to <MEMORY_ROOT>/config/secrets.env and run `chmod 600` on the
# copy. launchd/run-with-env.sh sources it at service start and refuses a file
# that group or other can read. Nothing in here ever belongs in a plist.
#
# Every line ships commented out, so copying this file as-is sets nothing and
# every built-in default stays in force. Uncomment and fill in only the lines
# for the services you run; an uncommented empty line still exports an empty
# value, which can mask a default. Values are plain shell assignments: quote
# anything that contains spaces. Illustrative values below are synthetic.
#
# Gemini keys (GEMINI_API_KEY, GEMINI_API_KEYS) do not belong here: no service
# reads them. Only the MCP server's reranker uses them, from the `env` block of
# the MCP client registration, and the manual scripts/backfill-embeddings.mjs
# from the shell you run it in.

# slack-connector: user token the connector reads your workspaces with.
# SLACK_USER_TOKEN=

# slack-connector: comma-separated workspace ids to ingest.
# SLACK_WORKSPACE_IDS=

# git-log-connector: colon-separated author addresses that count as you, such as alex@example.com:alex@example.org
# GIT_LOG_OPERATOR_EMAILS=

# telegram capture: numeric api id of your own Telegram application.
# TELEGRAM_API_ID=

# telegram capture: api hash that pairs with TELEGRAM_API_ID.
# TELEGRAM_API_HASH=

# telegram capture: absolute path of the session file telegram_login.py creates.
# TELEGRAM_SESSION_FILE=

# telegram capture: a login session as a string instead of a file. It is a full
# credential for your account and wins over TELEGRAM_SESSION_FILE when both are
# set. Uncomment only if you use it.
# TELEGRAM_SESSION_STRING=
